Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Period live-action. BUCK, a massive four-year-old dog with a broad St. Bernard chest, shepherd-dog muzzle, long dark coat, brown markings above his eyes, and a white blaze on his chest, rules the sunny Santa Clara Valley estate.

Buck plunges into the cement swimming tank, races beside the Judge's grandsons, and lies before the library fire at JUDGE MILLER'S feet.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was king--king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck through the orchard and ties a stout rope beneath his collar. A STRANGER waits beside a dark baggage car.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The Stranger tightens the rope. Buck lunges, snarling, but is choked down and thrown into the baggage car as the train shrieks away.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

Buck lies battered inside a cagelike crate. The SALOON-KEEPER peers through its bars by candlelight while the KIDNAPPER unwraps his bleeding hand.

KIDNAPPER
All I get is fifty for it, an' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty. And he's worth it, or I'm a squarehead.

Buck springs at the bars whenever the door opens, hoping for Judge Miller. Each hope becomes a savage growl.

EXT. DOG-BREAKER'S YARD, SEATTLE - DAY

A stout DOG-BREAKER in a sagging red sweater splits Buck's crate with a hatchet. Four CARRIERS climb the wall to watch.

Buck bursts through the opening, one hundred and forty pounds of bloodshot fury. The Dog-Breaker's club catches him in midair and hurls him down.

Buck charges again and again. Each time the club drops him harder, until blood stains his long coat and he crumples unconscious.

The Dog-Breaker gives Buck water and raw meat from his hand when he wakes.

DOG-BREAKER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that.

DOG-BREAKER
You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

PERRAULT, a small French-Canadian courier, leads Buck and CURLY aboard. FRANCOIS, a tall, black-faced French-Canadian half-breed, takes their leashes.

Below deck wait SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with an easy, treacherous grin, and DAVE, a gloomy, battle-scarred husky.

Francois's whip cracks when Spitz steals Buck's food. Spitz yields the bone.

Buck watches, learning the difference between a fair master and a thief.

EXT. DYE A BEACH - DAY

Buck steps off the ship onto snow. His paws sink into the white surface. He sniffs it, licks it, and leaps back as it bites his tongue and vanishes.

The men laugh. Buck stares at the falling snow, ashamed and bewildered.

EXT. DYE A BEACH CAMP - DAY

Curly approaches a wolfish HUSKY with friendly curiosity. In a flash, the husky rips her face from eye to jaw.

Curly rushes him. He throws her off her feet.

A silent ring of huskies closes over Curly. Francois swings an axe and Perrault a club, driving them away too late. Curly lies dead in the trampled snow.

Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue in laughter. Buck fixes on him with a cold, permanent hatred.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you.

EXT. DYE A BEACH CAMP - NIGHT

Francois harnesses Buck between Dave and Sol-leks, an old one-eyed husky with a scarred face. Buck balks at the straps, then pulls a sled loaded with firewood.

DAVE nips Buck's hindquarters whenever he tangles the traces. SOL-LEKS slashes Buck when he approaches from his blind side.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pull lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

Later, Buck shivers outside the warm tent. He finds BILLEE buried beneath the snow, curled in a pocket of warm air.

Buck digs his own snow-hole and sleeps.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck leads the team through wind, deep snow, and thin river ice. His coat has grown rougher; his muscles harden under it.

PERRAULT probes ahead with a long pole. The ice breaks beneath the sled. Buck and Dave are dragged from black water, coated in ice.

Francois runs them around a fire until steam rises from their fur.

At camp, Buck gulps his fish before the others can steal it. When Perrault turns away, Buck takes a chunk of bacon. DUB, an awkward husky, is blamed and punished.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the Northland, under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Driving snow lashes the camp. Buck returns from his ration to find Spitz occupying his warm nest beneath a rock.

Buck attacks. Spitz circles him, teeth flashing.

Starving huskies surge from the darkness and raid the camp. They tear through food and dogs alike.

Buck seizes one raider by the throat. Spitz bites Buck from the side and escapes into the confusion.

Buck, Dave, Sol-leks, Billee, and the wounded team flee over the ice.

FRANCOIS
Mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites.

PERRAULT
Four hundred miles to Dawson. We go on.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit bolts from camp. Buck leads sixty dogs in a moonlit chase, running with a wild wolf-cry.

Spitz cuts across a bend, catches the rabbit, and breaks its back.

Buck crashes into Spitz shoulder to shoulder. The pack forms a silent circle around them.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and leaps clear. Buck bleeds, pants, and studies him.

Buck feints for Spitz's throat, then drops low. His teeth crush Spitz's left foreleg.

Spitz struggles on three legs. Buck repeats the trick and breaks the right foreleg.

The circle tightens. Buck makes the final rush. The pack closes over Spitz.

Buck stands in the moon-flooded snow, bloodied and upright.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - MORNING

Francois tries to put Sol-leks in the lead position. Buck drives Sol-leks aside and takes the place.

Francois raises his club. Buck keeps just beyond its reach, refusing to yield.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots immediately into the lead traces.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

The team races over hard snow behind Buck. Under his discipline, Pike pulls, Joe obeys, and the dogs leap together in the traces.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Buck and the team arrive gaunt and footsore. HAL, a beardless young man with a revolver belt, CHARLES, a watery-eyed middle-aged man with a fierce mustache, and MERCEDES, Charles's soft, pretty wife and Hal's sister, buy them.

Their camp is a chaos of unwashed dishes, blankets, and luggage. They heap everything onto a sled until it towers above the dogs.

The runners are frozen fast. Hal whips the exhausted team.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't. The poor dears!

HAL
They're lazy, I tell you.

A nearby PROSPECTOR points at the runners.

PROSPECTOR
Throw your weight against the gee-pole, right and left, and break it out.

Hal does. The sled lurches forward, overturns at the first corner, and scatters their belongings through the street.

EXT. WHITE RIVER BANK - DAY

Spring sun glitters on rotten river ice. Buck is skeletal, his long coat limp and matted with dried blood. Only SOL-LEKS, JOE, PIKE, and TEEK remain beside him.

JOHN THORNTON, a lean, weathered prospector with a rough beard and kind eyes, whittles an axe-handle outside his camp.

Hal raises a club over Buck, who cannot rise.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks Hal's knife away with the axe-handle, then cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes drive the remaining dogs onto the ice. Thornton kneels beside Buck and checks his bruised body.

The sled moves away. The ice opens beneath it. Dogs, sled, and humans disappear into black water.

Thornton looks at Buck.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

SKEET, a small Irish setter, cleans Buck's wounds with patient licks. NIG, a huge black half-bloodhound, invites Buck to play.

Buck's flesh returns. His coat gleams again. He lies at Thornton's feet, watching every movement of the man.

Thornton takes Buck's great head between his hands and shakes it affectionately.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck takes Thornton's hand gently between his teeth, leaving the print of his fangs without breaking skin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON STREET, DAWSON - DAY

A sled loaded with twenty sacks of flour stands frozen to the hard-packed snow. MATTHEWSON, a Bonanza King, lays a sack of gold dust on a barrel.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

HANS and PETE, Thornton's quiet partners, stand beside him. A crowd of MINERS gathers around Buck.

Thornton kneels, presses cheek to Buck's head, and whispers.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines and grips Thornton's mittened hand in his teeth.

Thornton steps back.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck pulls right. The runners crack.

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck pulls left. The sled pivots free.

THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck throws his whole body into the traces. His claws cut grooves in the snow. The sled jerks, inches, then moves steadily toward a pile of firewood.

The crowd erupts as Buck passes the marker.

Thornton falls to his knees beside him, shaking his head in both hands.

THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

A year later. Moose-hide sacks of gold stand outside a spruce-bough lodge. Thornton, Hans, and Pete sleep near their fire.

Buck, larger and more wolfish now, hears a long, lean timber wolf howl from the forest.

He slips into the trees.

EXT. FOREST CREEK BED - NIGHT

Buck finds the TIMBER WOLF, lean and gray, howling beneath the stars.

The wolf flees. Buck follows, then corners him at a timber jam. Instead of attacking, Buck circles with careful, friendly movements.

At last they touch noses. They run side by side through the moonlit forest.

At a stream, Buck stops. He remembers Thornton.

The Timber Wolf whines for him to follow. Buck turns back toward camp.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Buck returns from days in the wild, alert and powerful. He scents something wrong.

Nig lies dead in a thicket, pierced by an arrow. Farther on, Hans lies face-down, feathered with arrows.

Buck creeps to the ruined camp. YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wreckage of the lodge.

Buck sees Skeet dead at the edge of a muddy pool. Thornton's scent leads into the water and does not return.

Buck roars.

He tears through the hunters, ripping one throat, then another. Arrows fly wildly; the hunters panic and flee into the forest.

Buck pursues them until the woods are silent, then returns to the pool.

He stands over the water through the falling night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, as a cessation of movement, as a passing out and away from the lives of the living, he knew.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And he knew John Thornton was dead.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Moonlight fills the clearing. A wolf pack pours from the trees, silver shapes surrounding Buck.

The boldest wolf leaps. Buck strikes and breaks its neck.

The pack surges. Buck backs into a gravel angle beside the pool and faces them, snapping and turning with impossible speed.

After a long half-hour, the wolves draw back.

The Timber Wolf approaches, whining softly. Buck recognizes him. They touch noses.

An OLD WOLF, gaunt and battle-scarred, raises his muzzle and howls at the moon. The pack joins him.

Buck steps from the gravel angle and sits among them. He lifts his head and answers the call.

The wolves race into the forest. Buck runs at their head, his great throat raised in the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.
